Date: Fri, 15 May 2026 11:19:40 -0500
Subject: half-broken DNSSEC rapidcow.net

Hey y'all, so I'm trying out free secondary servers to
serve alongside my two measly Digital Ocean droplets.
rapidcow.net is the "lab rat" this time (which most of
you shouldn't know of; i only got it in a fit of anger
towards GoDaddy holding rapidcow.com hostage for some
unholy cost).  currently trying out BuddyNS (which i
keep misspelling as "bundy"...); apparently out of all
records in the world they don't support RRSIG!

Because DNSSEC is a paid feature?  I mean I'd get it
if they're the one rotating the keys and signing them,
but if I'm just letting you serve the RRSIG/NSEC3...
it shouldn't be any more work than being a RFC-compliant
secondary server. :(

Ironically directly below a retweet by Bundy^W BUDDY,
sorry, NS, the same person says:

https://twitter.com/jefhar70/status/1583566517429338112

> Actually I'm switching secondary dns from BuddyNS to
> http://dns.he.net.  One of them strips dnssec records
> from the slave zone, and the other preserves them.

https://twitter.com/jefhar70/status/1583596812585209856

> Well, they won't strip the dnssec records if you pay
> them, but that's like paying for a TLS certificate in
> the age of Let's Encrypt.

(I actually removed mentions of BuddyNS and letsencrypt
because i hate handle-centric internet.  That said, there
is a certain tinge of hope when there wasn't a handle for
he.net...)

Anyways, I'm dropping Buddy from the registry NS...
it's a good thing all the big recursive name servers
(1.1.1.1, 8.8.8.8, 9.9.9.9) didn't drop my name records
because of Buddy misbehaving... so much for that. [% FILTER html %]>n>[% END %]
